implementationPlan.description:
Land the module skeleton: apps/daemon/src/telemetry/provider-run-reader.ts plus its EXACT one-line LF bridge `export * from "./provider-run-reader.ts";` (a new .ts without its .js bridge is invisible to consumers while tsc and vitest both stay green).
Declare the NARROW READ-ONLY PORT here — task rail 2 forbids widening `ProviderRunStore`, which carries `commitExpectedVersionDecision` and must not become reachable from a reader:
```
export interface ProviderRunReadStore {
  getHealth(): StoreHealth;
  readEventHorizon(): bigint;
  readEventsByTypeAfter(t: string, after: bigint, limit?: number): CursorPage<StoredEvent, bigint>;
  getCommandDecision(key: CommandDecisionKey): CommandDecisionRecord | null;
  getCommandReceipt(commandId: string): CommandReceipt | null;
}
```
Five readers and no writer, so "this reader mutates nothing" is a property of the TYPE rather than a rule to remember — the same posture `ImportShadowStorePort` takes.
Entry: `readCurrentProviderRun(store, request)` with `request = {projectId, attemptRef}`. Validate both as query text; anything else -> `PROVIDER_RUN_EVIDENCE_MALFORMED` at the reader layer (storeCode null). Then `store.getHealth().projectId !== projectId` -> refuse; a throw anywhere in this stage -> `PROVIDER_RUN_EVIDENCE_UNREADABLE` carrying the store's own `DurableStoreError.code` verbatim in `storeCode`. Import the codes from `./provider-run-refusals.js`; mint none.
Declare explicit ceilings as named constants: a page limit and a total candidate ceiling. Both are bounds this module owns, and a ceiling breach is a refusal rather than a truncated answer — a silently truncated scan is how a duplicate hides.
